---
// The home page, built from the approved design (design/DESIGN.md) and the
// order of #329: the concrete result and what AI and Codeyard each do, the
// coding tools and the fact line, why the open decisions come first, the
// gym scenario of #328 in four steps, who does what, one real case, who it
// is for, and the ways on. The first layer uses plain words; a technical term
// follows its plain name. The full route with its risk levels and profiles is
// on the workflow page; script names and the terminal start are in the docs.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import { site } from "../../site.config";
import { href } from "../lib/links";

const [first, ...rest] = site.tagline.split(/(?<=,) /);
const second = rest.join(" ");

// What can happen when a coding tool works on its own, and how Codeyard
// handles it, row by row and about as long on both sides.
const compare = [
  { topic: "Open decisions", without: "The tool fills a decision you left open with a choice of its own.", with: "You answer a few questions first, and the answers are written down as agreements." },
  { topic: "Long work", without: "A new session does not know what was agreed or done before.", with: "The plan and the progress are files that the next session reads." },
  { topic: "Checking", without: "The tool that wrote a change is the one that judges it.", with: `By default, a tool of another provider <a href="${href("features/#review")}">reviews it</a>, and the checks run first.` },
  { topic: "Is it done?", without: "A change can look finished while part of it is missing.", with: "Each agreement counts as met only with what shows it." },
];
---
<Base description={site.description}>
  <div class="hero wrap">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <h1>{first} <em>{second}</em></h1>
      <div class="hero-side">
        <p class="lede">Describe what you want to build. Agent Codeyard helps you clarify what you need, plan the work, and guide AI coding tools as they build and check each change. You review the result and decide whether to accept it.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn" href={href("workflow/")}>See how it works</a>
          <a href={href("docs/getting-started/")}>Get started</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-copy">Claude Code and Codex are AI coding tools: from a description, they write the code and its tests. Codeyard writes no code. It organises their work: questions first, then a plan, a role for each tool, checks on every change, and a stop wherever you are needed.</p>
    <p class="facts-line"><span>Open source</span><span>Runs on your machine</span><span>The coding tools talk to their providers</span><span>You decide what is accepted</span></p>
  </div>

  <Section id="why" title="Why not start building straight away?">
    <Fragment slot="note">What each part adds: <a href={href("features/")}>Why Codeyard</a>.</Fragment>
    <p>AI coding tools can start building from your first request. But a request often leaves important decisions open. Agent Codeyard gives those decisions a place in the process, before they become assumptions in the code.</p>
    <p>Claude Code and Codex can plan, ask questions, run tests and support a review by themselves. Codeyard makes one process of it: the agreements are written down before the building starts, each tool has an assigned role, the checks run on every change, and the progress is recorded, so that the next session can continue from it.</p>
    <div class="compare rows" role="group" aria-label="Possible problems and how Codeyard handles them" style={`--rows: ${compare.length + 1}`}>
      <div class="col"><h3>What can happen</h3><dl>
        {compare.map((row) => <div><dt>{row.topic}</dt><dd>{row.without}</dd></div>)}
      </dl></div>
      <div class="col"><h3>How Codeyard handles it</h3><dl>
        {compare.map((row) => <div><dt>{row.topic}</dt><dd set:html={row.with} /></div>)}
      </dl></div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="example" title="One example in four steps">
    <Fragment slot="note"><strong>Illustrative example.</strong> The questions, the check and the review finding are made up to show the steps. Every step in detail: <a href={href("workflow/")}>How it works</a>.</Fragment>
    <p>A gym wants an application for its members. Trainers record attendance for their classes; members see their own training history.</p>
    <ol class="words scenario-steps" aria-label="The four steps of the illustrative example">
      <li>
        <b>1. Clarify what you need</b>
        <dl class="facts">
          <dt>you do</dt><dd>Describe the idea in a few sentences, then answer questions, a few at a time.</dd>
          <dt>what happens</dt><dd>An AI tool asks what your description leaves open:
            <ul class="asked">
              <li>Who may record or correct attendance?</li>
              <li>May members see other members' data?</li>
              <li>What happens with a wrong registration?</li>
            </ul>
          </dd>
          <dt>what comes out</dt><dd>The agreements: trainers record and correct attendance for their own classes; a member sees only their own history; a correction keeps the original entry visible, marked as corrected.</dd>
          <dt>why it helps</dt><dd>You make these decisions before the code exists, instead of finding a guess in it later. Technically, this round is the Project Grill, and the agreements become the requirements.</dd>
        </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>2. Plan the work</b>
        <dl class="facts">
          <dt>you do</dt><dd>Read the plan and approve it.</dd>
          <dt>what happens</dt><dd>The work is put in order: users and roles first, then classes and attendance, then the member's history view.</dd>
          <dt>what comes out</dt><dd>Small features, each with the agreements it must meet.</dd>
          <dt>why it helps</dt><dd>Each part builds on the one before and can be checked on its own.</dd>
        </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>3. Build and check</b>
        <dl class="facts">
          <dt>you do</dt><dd>Start a feature, and answer if a tool stops to ask.</dd>
          <dt>what happens</dt><dd>Claude Code builds the attendance part and its tests. A check confirms that a member who asks for another member's history is refused. By default, Codex then reads the change without editing it: here it finds that the first version let every signed-in user open the history view, and the fix is checked again.</dd>
          <dt>what comes out</dt><dd>A proposed change, with the checks that ran and the review's remarks.</dd>
          <dt>why it helps</dt><dd>The change is checked against what you agreed before you look at it.</dd>
        </dl>
      </li>
      <li class="you">
        <b>4. Decide</b>
        <dl class="facts">
          <dt>you do</dt><dd>Read what was made, which checks ran and what the review found, and accept the change or send it back.</dd>
          <dt>what happens</dt><dd>Nothing becomes part of the project before you accept it. Technically, the change is a pull request, and accepting it is the merge.</dd>
          <dt>what comes out</dt><dd>The attendance part in your application, or a change you did not take.</dd>
          <dt>why it helps</dt><dd>The last decision about your project stays with you.</dd>
        </dl>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section id="roles" title="Who does what">
    <Fragment slot="note">Where you are needed, step by step: <a href={href("workflow/")}>How it works</a>.</Fragment>
    <div class="ledger" role="group" aria-label="Who does what">
      <div><h3>You</h3><ul><li>Describe what you want</li><li>Answer the questions and approve the plan</li><li>Answer when a tool stops to ask</li><li>Accept or reject each change</li></ul></div>
      <div><h3>The coding tools</h3><ul><li>Ask the questions and draft the plan</li><li>Write the code and its tests</li><li>Review a change another tool wrote</li><li>Fix what the review found</li></ul></div>
      <div><h3>Codeyard's scripts</h3><ul><li>Start each tool in its role, with its instructions</li><li>Run the checks and hold back unfinished work</li><li>Have a tool of another provider review by default</li><li>Record each run, so that work can continue</li></ul></div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="projects" title="A real project">
    <Fragment slot="note">The whole case, with its sources: <a href={href("examples/#application")}>Projects</a>.</Fragment>
    <ol class="story">
      <li><b>The project.</b> Physics by Construction, a course by the maintainer, runs this workflow.</li>
      <li><b>The problem.</b> Its tests started hundreds of processes to read the lessons, and one of them crashed now and then.</li>
      <li><b>The change.</b> One agreed request: read the lessons another way, with six conditions for when it is done.</li>
      <li><b>The review.</b> Four rounds. One finding: a new test could skip itself, so the change needed the owner's approval.</li>
      <li><b>The decision.</b> The owner accepted the change the same day. <a href={href("examples/#application")}>The case</a></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="needs">The other projects are features of Codeyard itself, built with its own workflow, such as <a href={href("examples/#memory")}>the reviewer who did not know what was decided</a> and <a href={href("examples/#story")}>how these pages were built</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="who" title="Who it is for">
    <Fragment slot="note">Skills, cost and when it pays off: the <a href={href("faq/")}>FAQ</a>.</Fragment>
    <p>Understanding Codeyard takes no programming. Using it does: you work in a terminal, with Git and a GitHub account. It is not a no-code tool. <a href={href("faq/#who")}>What you need to know</a></p>
    <p>You need Claude Code and Codex, each with its own subscription or paid API use: as shipped, Claude Code builds and Codex reviews. Codeyard itself is free and open source. <a href={href("faq/#cost")}>What it costs</a></p>
    <p>The questions, the plan, the checks and the reviews take time and model use. That is worth it for software that will be extended and maintained; for a small experiment, a coding tool on its own is quicker. <a href={href("faq/#too-heavy")}>When it is too much</a></p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="start" title="Start, or read further">
    <Fragment slot="note">New to coding tools? <a href={href("docs/new-to-agentic-coding/")}>New to agentic coding</a>.</Fragment>
    <div class="split">
      <div><h3>Start</h3><ul>
        <li>A new project: <a href={href("docs/getting-started/")}>Getting started</a></li>
        <li>A project you already have: <a href={href("docs/adopting/")}>Adopting Codeyard</a></li>
      </ul></div>
      <div><h3>Read further</h3><ul>
        <li>Every step and its technical detail: <a href={href("workflow/")}>How it works</a></li>
        <li>What each part adds, and what the scripts enforce: <a href={href("features/")}>Why Codeyard</a></li>
        <li>Every command and setting: <a href={href("docs/")}>Docs</a></li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
